                <pb facs="0304" n="206" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg206"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/11272: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Hankow</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Lockhart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Hankow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-10-28T13:00:00+08:00">October 28, 1930—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 28—8:50 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>The following telegram has been sent to the Legation: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“October 28, 3 p.m. U. S. S. <hi rendition="#italic">Luzon</hi> silenced
                            fieldpiece on left bank of the river at mileage 166 above Hankow
                            yesterday. Many Red troops of [<hi rendition="#italic">all?</hi>] along
                            river bank. Naval authorities report Kienli practically deserted but
                            river quiet between that point and mileage 215.</p>
                        <p>Nanchow, Hunan, was captured by communist bandits October 22. Hwayung,
                            Hunan, also captured, communist bandits operating area numbering 4,000
                            to 1,000 [<hi rendition="#italic">5,000?</hi>]. Two Americans, Miss
                            Hazel Todd and W. W. Moore, who were reported as being there are in
                            Hsucheng (Anhwei) and Kuling, respectively.</p>
                        <p>Sixty-first Division arrived here yesterday and today and immediately
                            transferred to Wuchang.</p>
                        <p>Repeated to the Department.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lockhart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
